From a7ffad5efc64efe10f42e7f009b86d9698bfe09b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Groboclown <groboclown@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 17:21:28 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 2/5] proxy: deliver secrets after startup over an inject
 socket

Add `[secrets] source = "inject"`. The proxy starts with no service token
and an empty, pending store, and listens on a 0600 Unix socket. The new
`seekrit-proxy inject --socket PATH` subcommand sends one JSON payload
from stdin: either {"token": "skt_..."} (resolved and decrypted through
the API as at startup) or {"values": {"NAME": "..."}} (used verbatim).

What it prevents:

- A token or secret in the container's definition. With the token in
  SEEKRIT_TOKEN or --token, anyone who can `docker inspect` the container
  reads it; a mounted secrets file is readable with `docker cp`. Injected
  over a `docker exec -i` stdin, the payload only exists in memory.
- Forwarding a placeholder before the secrets arrive. Until the load, a
  permitted placeholder is refused with 503 ("secrets not loaded yet"),
  distinct from the 403 for a denied or unknown secret. Requests without
  placeholders are served as usual.
- Replacing the secrets later. The first successful load closes and
  unlinks the socket; a failed load changes nothing and may be retried.
  Connections are handled one at a time with a read timeout, and only the
  proxy's own uid (kernel-attested) is admitted.
- Leaking values. Replies and logs carry counts and names only; payload
  errors describe the shape and never quote the input; payload buffers
  are zeroized and sized up front.

Inject mode refuses configs that would need the token at startup
([policy] source = "server", [tasks], [activity], [cache], and
[secrets] refresh_interval), refuses a token passed alongside it, and
checks the socket path is absolute and fits a Unix socket address.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
